[h1]Lizarb Interface[/h1]

A complete reskin of RimWorld 1.6's interface. Window frames, buttons, tabs, tooltips, checkboxes, gizmos, scrollbars, fonts and the Architect menu.

[h2]Presets[/h2]

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dequevedo/lizarb-interface/main/docs/themes.png[/img]

Pick one and the whole interface changes at once: the textures, the typeface and its sizes, the text outline, the background pattern and its strength, and how the Architect menu is coloured.

A preset is not just a skin. Two presets can draw with the same textures and read completely differently, which is what separates PixelStone from PixelStone Fake: the same pixels, one with a pixel typeface and no background, one without.

Change anything and save it as a preset of your own. The texture sets are grouped: squared ones that sit closer to the game's own look, rounded ones marked experimental, and hand painted ones.

The first entry is [b]RimWorld default[/b], which hands every surface and the font back to the game.

[h2]Make your own[/h2]

Drop a folder into the mod's Skins directory with a single ButtonBG.png in it and it shows up in the list, with no code and no config. Anything you do not draw is filled in from your own files first, then from a complete set, so one file is already enough and a full set is a finished one.

[h2]Fonts[/h2]

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dequevedo/lizarb-interface/main/docs/fonts.png[/img]

Change the font the whole game is drawn in. The picker draws every name in its own face, so you pick by looking. Anything installed on your machine shows up too.

Text sizes are adjustable, and every label can carry a real outline.

[h2]Settings[/h2]

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dequevedo/lizarb-interface/main/docs/shapes.png[/img]

Shapes for the Architect colour, shown here, plus the opening animation, the gap between elements, the strength of the background texture, whether small text gets an outline, and a switch for each surface the mod touches.

[h2]The Architect menu[/h2]

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dequevedo/lizarb-interface/main/docs/architect.png[/img]

Category buttons get a colour and an icon, so you find the one you want by sight instead of by reading. Categories from other mods get one too. Pick the shape, or turn it off.

[h2]Compatibility[/h2]

Load this one last if you want its look to win over another UI mod.

Buttons, window frames, tabs, widgets and scrollbars can each be handed back to the game on their own, so a clash costs one checkbox instead of the whole skin.

[h2]Built with AI[/h2]

The code and this description were written with Claude, Anthropic's assistant, with me directing, testing and deciding.

The textures are not generated images. They are drawn by the generator scripts in the repository, from geometry, and you can read and run them yourself.

